  Val loss started rising at epoch 18 and NEVER recovered.
  13 consecutive epochs of val loss above the epoch 17 minimum.
  The rise decelerated significantly after epoch 24 (delta < 0.01 per epoch).
  Final val loss (1.2749) is 6.4% above best Phase 2e val loss (1.1985).
  Final val loss (1.2749) is 7.1% above best overall val loss (1.1901 from Phase 1).

Critical observation: Phase 2e's best val loss (1.1985, epoch 17) never
matched Phase 1's best (1.1901, epoch 15). The model achieved higher ACCURACY
at worse LOSS. This means Phase 2e improved discrimination (correct class gets
highest logit) while degrading calibration (confidence probabilities are less
accurate).

============================================================
6. LR SCHEDULE CORRELATION
============================================================

Phase 1: CosineAnnealingLR, T_max=15, peak LR=1e-4, warmup=2 epochs
  - Epochs 1-2 (warmup, 1e-6 to 5e-5): Model adapted from pretrained features.
    Val accuracy jumped from 1.25% to 70.50%. Val loss dropped from 5.32 to 2.18.
  - Epochs 3-7 (peak to mid decay, 1e-4 to 7.9e-5): Steady accuracy gains.
    Val accuracy: 77.75% to 87.62%.
  - Epochs 8-15 (mid to low decay, 6.8e-5 to 2.4e-6): Diminishing returns.
    Val accuracy: 87.12% to 90.87%. Val loss: 1.27 to 1.19.
    The last 5 epochs gained only 1.37pp -- LR was too low for major moves.

Phase 2e: CosineAnnealingLR, T_max=15, peak LR=5e-6, warmup=1 epoch
  - Epoch 16 (warmup, 5e-8): Near-zero LR. Val accuracy dipped to 90.12%.
    Train loss was higher (1.17) due to regularization at near-zero LR.
  - Epoch 17 (peak, 5e-6): Best val loss (1.1985). Val accuracy matched Phase 1 (90.87%).
  - Epochs 18-19 (early decay, 4.95e-6 to 4.80e-6): Main improvement window.
    Val accuracy peaked at 91.75% (epoch 19).
  - Epochs 20-30 (decay, 4.56e-6 to 1.05e-06): No accuracy improvement.
    Val loss rose monotonically. Accuracy plateaued at ~91.25-91.50%.

Correlation: The useful learning in Phase 2e happened in a 3-epoch window
(epochs 17-19) at peak LR. Once cosine decay began reducing LR, no further
accuracy gains materialized. This suggests that 5e-6 was the minimum LR
needed to push past the Phase 1 plateau, and the remaining 11 epochs were
wasted compute with only negative effects on calibration.

============================================================
7. EARLY STOPPING ANALYSIS
============================================================

Early stopping patience was set to 15 (effectively disabled for a 15-epoch run).

If early stopping had been set by val top-1 accuracy:
  - Patience 3: Would have stopped at epoch 22 (3 epochs after best at 19)
    Final model: epoch 19, 91.75% -- same as actual best
  - Patience 5: Would have stopped at epoch 24
  - Patience 10: Would have stopped at epoch 29
  - Patience 15: Ran to completion (epoch 30)

If early stopping had been set by val loss:
  - Patience 3: Would have stopped at epoch 20 (3 epochs after best loss at 17)
    Final model: epoch 17, 90.87% -- would have MISSED the 91.75% at epoch 19
    IMPORTANT: Loss-based early stopping would have been harmful here.
  - Patience 5: Would have stopped at epoch 22
  - Patience 10: Would have stopped at epoch 27

Recommendation: For this model/dataset, early stopping by val top-1 accuracy
with patience=5 would be optimal. It captures the best model while avoiding
the wasted 11 epochs of overfitting. Loss-based stopping is unreliable because
of the accuracy-loss divergence observed in Phase 2e.

Compute saved with patience=5: 6 epochs * 168s = ~17 minutes (39% of Phase 2e runtime).

============================================================
8. CALIBRATION ANALYSIS
============================================================

Val loss vs val accuracy movement:

Phase 1 (coherent):
  Both val loss and val accuracy improved together throughout.
  As val loss decreased (5.32 -> 1.19), accuracy increased (1.25% -> 90.87%).
  Calibration was healthy in Phase 1.

Phase 2e (divergent):
  Epoch 17: Best val loss (1.1985), accuracy 90.87% -- calibration aligned
  Epoch 19: Val loss 1.2291 (+2.6%), accuracy 91.75% (+0.88pp) -- diverging
  Epoch 30: Val loss 1.2749 (+6.4%), accuracy 91.37% (+0.50pp) -- fully diverged

  The model learned to assign higher probability to the correct class
  (improving top-1 accuracy) while simultaneously becoming more overconfident
  on incorrect predictions (increasing cross-entropy loss).

  This is a known effect of continued fine-tuning with:
    - MixUp/CutMix (creates soft boundary regions that confuse calibration)
    - Label smoothing (the 0.1 smoothing fights against the model's increasing
      confidence, inflating loss on correctly classified examples)
    - Low LR fine-tuning (the model sharpens existing decision boundaries
      rather than learning new features, which hurts calibration)

  Val Top-5 was essentially constant at 98.75-99.00% throughout Phase 2e,
  confirming the model's ranking quality is excellent -- the correct class
  is almost always in the top 5. The calibration issue is purely about the
  confidence probability distribution, not about ranking.

============================================================
9. COMPARISON TO PHASE 1
============================================================

Phase 1 (output_20260314_031959):
  Model: ConvNeXt V2 Base | 384px
  LR: 1e-4 peak, cosine, T_max=15, warmup=2
  Dropout: 0.20
  Epochs: 1-15
  Best: Epoch 15 | 90.87% top-1 | 98.38% top-5 | Val loss 1.1901
  Runtime: 898.9s (~15 min)

Phase 2e (output_20260314_063401):
  Model: ConvNeXt V2 Base | 384px | Batch 12
  LR: 5e-6 peak, cosine, T_max=15, warmup=1
  Dropout: 0.15 (reduced from 0.20)
  Epochs: 16-30 (resumed from Phase 1)
  Best: Epoch 19 | 91.75% top-1 | 98.75% top-5 | Val loss 1.2291
  Runtime: 2619.7s (~43.7 min)

Phase 2e gains:
  Val Top-1: +0.88pp (90.87% -> 91.75%)
  Val Top-5: +0.37pp (98.38% -> 98.75%)
  Val Loss: +0.039 WORSE (1.1901 -> best 1.1985, then rising to 1.2749)

Phase 2e tradeoffs:
  - Accuracy improved but val loss degraded (calibration lost)
  - Reduced dropout (0.20 -> 0.15) likely contributed to faster overfitting
  - 20x lower peak LR meant the optimizer could only make small adjustments
  - Of 15 Phase 2e epochs, only 3 were productive (17-19)

Cost-benefit: 43.7 minutes of compute for +0.88pp accuracy is marginal.
The improvement is real but the efficiency was low. A shorter Phase 2e
(5-7 epochs with patience=5) would have achieved the same result in ~15 min.

============================================================
10. RECOMMENDATIONS FOR PHASE 3
============================================================

Current standing: 91.75% val top-1 on CUB-200-2011

A. Resolution increase (recommended first):
   - Move from 384px to 448px
   - Fine-grained bird classification benefits greatly from higher resolution
   - Will need batch_size reduction (12 -> 8 or 10) for RTX 4070 Super 12GB
   - Expected gain: +1-2pp based on CUB-200 literature
   - Use the epoch 19 best checkpoint as starting point

B. Stronger regularization to combat overfitting:
   - Increase dropout back to 0.20 or 0.25
   - Increase mixup_alpha to 0.3-0.4
   - Add RandAugment or stronger color augmentation
   - Consider stochastic depth if not already enabled

C. LR strategy:
   - Use an even lower peak LR: 2e-6 to 3e-6
   - Shorter schedule: 5-7 epochs total with patience=5 by accuracy
   - Warmup: 0 or 1 epoch (the model is already well-adapted)
   - Consider flat LR + decay-on-plateau instead of cosine

D. Test-time augmentation (TTA):
   - Average predictions over 5-10 augmented views
   - Expected gain: +0.5-1.0pp with no training cost
   - Can be applied to current best checkpoint immediately

E. Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA):
   - Average weights from epochs 17-22 (before severe overfitting)
   - May recover some calibration while maintaining accuracy

F. Model ensemble (if pursuing highest accuracy):
   - Train Phase 3 with 448px resolution
   - Ensemble Phase 2e best (384px) + Phase 3 best (448px)
   - Expected ensemble gain: +1-2pp over single model

G. Data-side improvements:
   - Use official CUB-200 train/test split for standard evaluation
   - Leverage bounding box annotations for crop-based training
   - Consider cleaning mislabeled images (find_mislabels.py output)

Priority order: D (free), A+C (next training run), B (if overfitting persists),
E (post-training), F+G (for final push)

